</section>

<section id="order">
  <div class="eyebrow">The answer</div>
  <h2>Suggested web-options order</h2>
  <p>On balanced scoring, <strong>Mamata Khammam now leads (75.2), with Kamineni close behind (73.8)</strong>. Khammam leads by more if you weight clinical and PG factors. So moving is mostly about getting to Hyderabad. If Hyderabad matters to you, this is the order I'd use:</p>
  <div class="order">
    <div class="slot"><span class="pos">OPTION 1</span><span class="name">Kamineni Academy, LB Nagar</span><p>Best mix of Hyderabad location and quality. 97.3% Final MBBS Part-I pass rate (Nov 2025), the strongest PG demand of the group (MD Gen Med closed at NEET-PG rank 18,272), and DM Cardio, MCh Neuro and MCh Uro on campus.</p></div>
    <div class="slot"><span class="pos">OPTION 2</span><span class="name">Apollo, Jubilee Hills</span><p>Most sought after by NEET-UG toppers and the best address and brand. But NMC renewed it only conditionally for 2025–26 (11 of 20 departments short of faculty), and PG aspirants rank its departments low. Swap it with Kamineni if brand and an abroad pathway matter most to you.</p></div>
    <div class="slot"><span class="pos">OPTION 3 · only if Hyderabad is decisive</span><span class="name">Mamata Academy, Bachupally</span><p>Same management as Khammam. Big, busy hospital (1,602 OPD a day) and the best intern stipend (₹22,527). But it only opened in 2019, is still NMC <em>Permitted</em> rather than Recognised, and scores below Khammam on every lens. List it only if being in Hyderabad matters more to you than anything else.</p></div>
    <div class="slot skip"><span class="pos">DON'T LIST</span><span class="name">SVS, Mahabubnagar</span><p>It scores level with Khammam but has no clear edge: 2023 pass rates of 75–93%, a ₹2,000 intern stipend, and you'd still be ~100 km from Hyderabad. Not worth losing your current seat.</p></div>
    <div class="slot held"><span class="pos">YOUR CURRENT SEAT</span><span class="name">Mamata, Khammam</span><p>Your fallback. Leave it off the list; you keep it if nothing above gets allotted. Not listing anything at all is also a defensible choice.</p></div>
  </div>
</section>

<section id="matrix">
  <div class="eyebrow">20 parameters</div>
  <h2>Parameter-by-parameter scorecard</h2>
  <div class="toolbar">
    <div class="legend">Score shading:
      <span class="sw" style="background:var(--h1)"></span>≤4
      <span class="sw" style="background:var(--h2)"></span>5–6
      <span class="sw" style="background:var(--h3)"></span>7
      <span class="sw" style="background:var(--h4)"></span>8
      <span class="sw" style="background:var(--h5)"></span>9–10 · outlined = best in row · small number = rank among 5
    </div>
    <button class="toggle" id="evtoggle" aria-pressed="true">Hide evidence</button>
  </div>
  <div class="tablewrap"><table id="tbl"></table></div>
</section>

<section id="passrates">
  <div class="eyebrow">Added parameter</div>
  <h2>MBBS university pass rates, 2021–2025</h2>
  <p class="muted" style="font-size:14px">Each figure is the share of students who appeared for a KNRUHS exam and passed it, taken from the college's own NMC-mandated disclosure. The <strong>2024–25 batch, 1st-year</strong> column comes from a college-wise KNRUHS list you shared (handwritten copy, not yet checked against the official result). None of the five colleges publishes a complete five-year history, so gaps are shown as gaps rather than filled with estimates. For context, KNRUHS reported <strong>92.25%</strong> passing Final MBBS statewide in March 2023.</p>
  <div class="tablewrap"><table class="pr">
    <thead><tr><th>College</th><th>2021</th><th>2022</th><th>2023</th><th>2024</th><th>2025</th><th>1st yr, 2024–25 batch</th><th>Disclosure</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><th>Apollo</th><td><b>99%</b> Final Pt-II (2016 batch)<br><b>80%</b> 1st yr (2019 batch)</td><td class="gap">not published</td><td><b>87%</b> 1st yr (134/170, 2021 batch)<br><b>97%</b> PG MD/MS</td><td class="gap">not published</td><td><b>85%</b> 2nd yr (125/147, May)<br><b>89%</b> Final Pt-I supply (31/35)</td><td class="new"><b>91.3%</b> · #1 of 26</td><td><span class="tag mid">Partial</span></td></tr>
      <tr><th>Kamineni, LB Nagar</th><td class="gap">not published</td><td class="gap">not published</td><td class="gap">not published</td><td class="gap">not published</td><td><b>97.3%</b> Final Pt-I (146/150, 2022 batch, Nov); 60 first classes</td><td class="new"><b>80.6%</b> · #12 of 26</td><td><span class="tag low">Latest only</span></td></tr>
      <tr><th>Mamata Academy</th><td colspan="5" class="gap">Nothing else published. The first batch (2019 entry) graduated in 2025, so there's no multi-year record yet.</td><td class="new"><b>84.6%</b> · #6 of 26</td><td><span class="tag low">Latest only</span></td></tr>
      <tr><th>SVS</th><td class="gap">not published</td><td class="gap">not published</td><td><b>75%</b> 1st yr (140/186)<br><b>76%</b> 2nd yr (104/137)<br><b>76%</b> Final Pt-I (78/102)<br><b>93%</b> Final Pt-II (91/98)<br><b>94%</b> PG MD/MS</td><td class="gap">not published</td><td class="gap">not published</td><td class="new"><b>77.3%</b> · #16 of 26</td><td><span class="tag mid">One full year</span></td></tr>
      <tr><th class="heldname">Mamata Khammam</th><td class="gap">Result sheet posted only as an image</td><td class="gap">not published</td><td class="gap">not published</td><td class="gap">not published</td><td class="gap">not published</td><td class="new"><b>84.5%</b> · #7 of 26</td><td><span class="tag low">Latest only</span></td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table></div>
  <div class="league">
    <h3>1st-year pass rate, 2024–25 batch: all 26 private colleges</h3>
    <p class="muted" style="font-size:13px">Your five colleges are highlighted. The median across the 26 colleges is 79.5% and the mean is 77.9%. The bar scale starts at 50%. SVS and Patnam Mahender Reddy are tied at 77.3%.</p>
    <ol class="lg"><li class="me"><span class="lr">1</span><span class="ln">Apollo, Hyderabad</span><span class="lb"><i style="width:82.6%"></i></span><span class="lv">91.3%</span></li><li class=""><span class="lr">2</span><span class="ln">Shadan</span><span class="lb"><i style="width:80.0%"></i></span><span class="lv">90%</span></li><li class=""><span class="lr">3</span><span class="ln">Father Colombo</span><span class="lb"><i style="width:77.3%"></i></span><span class="lv">88.66%</span></li><li class=""><span class="lr">4</span><span class="ln">Deccan, Hyderabad</span><span class="lb"><i style="width:73.2%"></i></span><span class="lv">86.6%</span></li><li class=""><span class="lr">5</span><span class="ln">CMR</span><span class="lb"><i style="width:72.0%"></i></span><span class="lv">86%</span></li><li class="me"><span class="lr">6</span><span class="ln">Mamata Academy, Bachupally</span><span class="lb"><i style="width:69.2%"></i></span><span class="lv">84.6%</span></li><li class="held"><span class="lr">7</span><span class="ln">Mamata Khammam</span><span class="lb"><i style="width:69.0%"></i></span><span class="lv">84.5%</span></li><li class=""><span class="lr">8</span><span class="ln">Kamineni, Narketpally</span><span class="lb"><i style="width:68.0%"></i></span><span class="lv">84%</span></li><li class=""><span class="lr">9</span><span class="ln">Arundathi</span><span class="lb"><i style="width:64.0%"></i></span><span class="lv">82%</span></li><li class=""><span class="lr">10</span><span class="ln">Dr VRK Women's</span><span class="lb"><i style="width:64.0%"></i></span><span class="lv">82%</span></li><li class=""><span class="lr">11</span><span class="ln">Bhaskara</span><span class="lb"><i style="width:62.6%"></i></span><span class="lv">81.3%</span></li><li class="me"><span class="lr">12</span><span class="ln">Kamineni, LB Nagar</span><span class="lb"><i style="width:61.2%"></i></span><span class="lv">80.6%</span></li><li class=""><span class="lr">13</span><span class="ln">RVM</span><span class="lb"><i style="width:59.2%"></i></span><span class="lv">79.6%</span></li><li class=""><span class="lr">14</span><span class="ln">MNR</span><span class="lb"><i style="width:58.6%"></i></span><span class="lv">79.3%</span></li><li class=""><span class="lr">15</span><span class="ln">Mahavir</span><span class="lb"><i style="width:57.2%"></i></span><span class="lv">78.6%</span></li><li class="me"><span class="lr">16</span><span class="ln">SVS, Mahabubnagar</span><span class="lb"><i style="width:54.6%"></i></span><span class="lv">77.3%</span></li><li class=""><span class="lr">17</span><span class="ln">Patnam Mahender Reddy</span><span class="lb"><i style="width:54.6%"></i></span><span class="lv">77.3%</span></li><li class=""><span class="lr">18</span><span class="ln">Surabhi</span><span class="lb"><i style="width:53.3%"></i></span><span class="lv">76.66%</span></li><li class=""><span class="lr">19</span><span class="ln">Maheshwara</span><span class="lb"><i style="width:45.2%"></i></span><span class="lv">72.6%</span></li><li class=""><span class="lr">20</span><span class="ln">Ayaan</span><span class="lb"><i style="width:45.0%"></i></span><span class="lv">72.5%</span></li><li class=""><span class="lr">21</span><span class="ln">Nova</span><span class="lb"><i style="width:42.6%"></i></span><span class="lv">71.3%</span></li><li class=""><span class="lr">22</span><span class="ln">Prathima Relief, Warangal</span><span class="lb"><i style="width:38.7%"></i></span><span class="lv">69.33%</span></li><li class=""><span class="lr">23</span><span class="ln">Prathima, Karimnagar</span><span class="lb"><i style="width:36.8%"></i></span><span class="lv">68.4%</span></li><li class=""><span class="lr">24</span><span class="ln">Mediciti</span><span class="lb"><i style="width:30.0%"></i></span><span class="lv">65%</span></li><li class=""><span class="lr">25</span><span class="ln">Chalmeda Anand Rao</span><span class="lb"><i style="width:21.0%"></i></span><span class="lv">60.5%</span></li><li class=""><span class="lr">26</span><span class="ln">TRR</span><span class="lb"><i style="width:12.0%"></i></span><span class="lv">56%</span></li></ol>
  </div>
  <div class="why">
    <h3>Why the numbers look the way they do</h3>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>First and second year are the hard filters everywhere.</strong> Under the CBME curriculum (from the 2019 batch), Anatomy, Physiology and Biochemistry, then Pathology, Pharmacology and Microbiology, eliminate the most students. Final Part-II results tend to run 90%+ because weak students have already been held back. That's why SVS shows 75% early and 93% at finals, and Apollo 80–87% in first year but 99% at finals.</li>
      <li><strong>"Appeared" includes repeaters.</strong> SVS's 186 first-year candidates against a 150-seat intake means about 36 students were retaking the exam, and repeaters pass at lower rates. That pulls SVS's headline percentage down more than a clean single-batch figure would.</li>
      <li><strong>Intake merit matters.</strong> Cat-A open seats closed at rank 41,663 at Apollo, 70–74k at Kamineni and Mamata Academy, and 1.18 lakh at SVS and 1.56 lakh at Khammam. Management seats close much deeper (Cat-B local closed around 2.9 lakh at SVS and 3.2 lakh at Khammam). A lower-scoring intake usually means more first-year failures unless the college makes up for it with teaching and internals.</li>
      <li><strong>Intake merit shows most clearly in first year.</strong> Apollo has the strongest intake of the five and tops all 26 colleges at 91.3%. Kamineni LB Nagar is the surprise: its intake is similar to Mamata Academy's, yet its first-year result is lower (80.6%, #12). Its 97.3% in Final Part-I suggests students who fall behind early do catch up by the final years.</li>
      <li><strong>The two Mamata campuses are level.</strong> Bachupally (84.6%) and Khammam (84.5%) are almost identical, even though Khammam's intake closes about 85,000 ranks deeper. That points to consistent teaching under the same management.</li>
      <li><strong>SVS is consistently below the median.</strong> At 77.3% this year and about 75% in 2023, its first-year results are below the 26-college median of 79.5%. Its repeater load and deeper intake both push the figure down.</li>
